::ENG3_U06_L06_Q01::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is preparing writing for a class LAN site.
What should guide the revision?{
~The number of colors on the page#Incorrect. Page color does not determine whether writing is ready for publication.
~The student's favorite image#Incorrect. Personal preference should not guide publication revision.
=The audience, purpose, clarity, evidence, and multimedia support#Correct. These elements determine whether writing is tailored and publication ready.
~The fastest way to export the file#Incorrect. Exporting quickly does not ensure quality writing.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q02::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A paragraph begins, "This shows things were hard."
Which revision is strongest for publication?{
=The narrator's description of the crowded room shows how pressure limits the character's choices.#Correct. This revision gives context, names the evidence, and explains the idea clearly.
~This shows a lot of hard things happened, which is important.#Incorrect. This is still vague and does not explain the evidence.
~Things were hard in the story and that is the main point.#Incorrect. The sentence lacks specific evidence and precise explanation.
~This is about hard things because hard things matter.#Incorrect. This repeats the idea without developing it for readers.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q03::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student wants to publish a literary analysis with multimedia.
Which plan is most appropriate?{
~Add unrelated images to make the page attractive#Incorrect. Multimedia must support understanding, not simply decorate.
~Export the first draft without checking conventions#Incorrect. Publication requires revision and quality control.
~Remove all evidence so the page is shorter#Incorrect. Literary analysis needs evidence.
=Revise the analysis, add a relevant captioned visual, and check conventions before export#Correct. This plan combines quality writing, audience awareness, multimedia, and publication readiness.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q04::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student identifies the audience as "students who have not read the text."
What should the published writing include?{
~Unexplained references to every character#Incorrect. Readers who have not read the text need context.
=Brief context before analysis so the audience can follow the claim#Correct. Audience-aware writing gives readers enough background to understand the analysis.
~Only quotations with no explanation#Incorrect. Readers need explanation of evidence.
~A conclusion but no introduction#Incorrect. The audience needs a clear entry point into the writing.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q05::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is selecting a title for published writing.
Which title is strongest?{
~My Stuff#Incorrect. This is too vague for a publication audience.
~Important Things About a Story#Incorrect. This gives little information about the specific topic.
=Conflict and Identity in the Final Scene#Correct. This title clearly signals the topic and analytical focus.
~English Assignment#Incorrect. This names the class task rather than the published purpose.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q06::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student includes a chart with no source note.
Why is this a publication issue?{
=Readers need to know where information comes from to trust the published work.#Correct. Source notes support credibility and responsible publication.
~Charts never need source information.#Incorrect. Charts based on information should be sourced.
~Source notes are only needed in printed books.#Incorrect. Online and LAN publications also need responsible source information.
~The chart should be hidden instead.#Incorrect. Hiding the chart does not address credibility or source use.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q07::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A draft has a claim, evidence, and explanation, but the ideas appear in random order.
What should the student revise?{
~The font color only#Incorrect. Font color does not fix organization.
=The structure so readers can follow the logic of the analysis#Correct. Publication-ready writing must be organized for audience understanding.
~The file name only#Incorrect. A file name does not fix the paragraph's organization.
~The topic so it becomes unrelated to the evidence#Incorrect. The topic should remain connected to the evidence.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q08::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student says, "The page is ready because it has a picture."
What is the best feedback?{
~A picture is the only requirement for publication.#Incorrect. Publication requires quality writing and purposeful media.
~The writing does not matter if the image is interesting.#Incorrect. The writing remains central to the publication.
~Export it immediately without reading it.#Incorrect. Final review is necessary before publication.
=Check whether the writing is revised and whether the picture helps the audience understand.#Correct. Both writing quality and multimedia purpose matter.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q09::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is preparing an online publication for classmates.
Which tone is most appropriate?{
~Careless and vague#Incorrect. A published piece should be clear and purposeful.
~Overly casual with unclear slang#Incorrect. Slang may confuse readers and weaken academic purpose.
=Clear, audience-aware, and academically appropriate#Correct. This tone supports publication quality and reader understanding.
~Angry and dismissive#Incorrect. This tone would not fit academic publication.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q10::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A paragraph uses the phrase "a lot of stuff" several times.
What revision best improves quality?{
=Replace vague language with precise words that fit the analysis.#Correct. Precise language improves clarity for the publication audience.
~Leave the phrase because readers can guess the meaning.#Incorrect. Published writing should reduce confusion, not rely on guessing.
~Add more vague phrases so the style is consistent.#Incorrect. Consistent vagueness does not improve quality.
~Remove all analysis from the paragraph.#Incorrect. Analysis is necessary; it should be made clearer.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q11::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student prepares to export writing.
Which step should happen before export?{
~Delete the title#Incorrect. A clear title helps readers understand the publication.
~Add random media at the last second#Incorrect. Multimedia should be purposeful and integrated.
~Ignore feedback because the draft is finished#Incorrect. Feedback supports revision and publication quality.
=Review the audience, clarity, multimedia, sources, and conventions#Correct. This final check helps ensure the writing is publication ready.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q12::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student wants to publish writing about a research question.
Which multimedia element best supports the publication?{
~A decorative icon unrelated to the topic#Incorrect. Decoration does not support the research purpose.
~A meme that changes the tone of the piece#Incorrect. The media should match the publication purpose and audience.
=A labeled graphic that summarizes the relationship between key findings#Correct. This graphic helps readers understand the research synthesis.
~A background image that makes the text hard to read#Incorrect. Media should not reduce readability.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q13::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student says, "I revised by changing the font."
What should the student do next?{
=Revise the content for clarity, organization, and audience understanding.#Correct. True revision improves the writing itself, not just its appearance.
~Stop because font changes count as full revision.#Incorrect. Formatting alone is not enough for quality writing.
~Remove all paragraph breaks.#Incorrect. Paragraph breaks often help readers follow ideas.
~Export without checking the writing.#Incorrect. Publication requires content and convention checks.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q14::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student includes evidence but no explanation.
Why is this a problem for publication?{
~Published writing should never include evidence.#Incorrect. Evidence is important in analytical publication.
~Readers should have to figure out all connections alone.#Incorrect. The writer is responsible for guiding readers.
=Readers may not understand how the evidence supports the claim.#Correct. Explanation is needed so the audience can follow the reasoning.
~The multimedia will automatically explain the evidence.#Incorrect. Multimedia can support explanation, but it does not replace the writer's reasoning.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q15::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student adapts an essay paragraph for a webpage.
Which change is most helpful?{
~Remove the claim so the paragraph feels less formal#Incorrect. The claim helps readers understand the purpose.
~Add unrelated links throughout the paragraph#Incorrect. Unrelated links distract from the writing.
~Use one very long block of text#Incorrect. Long blocks can make online reading harder.
=Use a clear title, focused paragraphing, and a relevant captioned visual#Correct. These choices improve online readability and audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q16::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A publication plan says the audience is "teachers reviewing my research."
Which detail should be emphasized?{
~Only entertainment value#Incorrect. Teachers reviewing research need clear claims, evidence, and source responsibility.
=Clear research claim, evidence, explanation, and source information#Correct. This matches the audience's need to evaluate academic quality.
~Unrelated personal stories#Incorrect. Unrelated material distracts from the research purpose.
~A page with images but no writing#Incorrect. The writing remains central to the publication.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q17::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student is preparing publication after peer feedback.
What is the best use of the feedback?{
~Ignore every comment because the writing belongs to the student#Incorrect. Feedback can help improve clarity and quality.
~Accept only comments about color#Incorrect. Content, organization, and clarity feedback are important.
=Revise unclear sections and check whether changes help the intended audience#Correct. Feedback should guide meaningful audience-aware revision.
~Delete the whole draft and start an unrelated topic#Incorrect. Revision should improve the existing writing unless the topic truly fails.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q18::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student publishes writing with many capitalization and punctuation errors.
What is the likely effect?{
~The errors make the writing more creative#Incorrect. Errors usually distract readers rather than improve quality.
=The errors may distract readers and reduce credibility.#Correct. Standard conventions help published writing look careful and trustworthy.
~The errors prove the multimedia is strong#Incorrect. Conventions and multimedia are separate quality checks.
~The errors do not matter in online writing#Incorrect. Online and LAN publications still require standard conventions.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q19::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student chooses a multimedia element after writing a paragraph.
What should the student ask?{
~Will this media make the page look crowded?#Incorrect. Crowding is a concern, but the stronger first question is purpose and audience understanding.
~Is this media the funniest option?#Incorrect. Humor may not fit the publication purpose.
~Can this media replace the paragraph?#Incorrect. Multimedia should support, not replace, quality writing.
=How does this media help readers understand the paragraph's claim or evidence?#Correct. This question checks whether multimedia is meaningfully integrated.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q20::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A published piece needs a lead sentence.
Which lead is strongest?{
~This is my project for English class.#Incorrect. This focuses on the assignment, not the published idea.
=A character's response to conflict can reveal the values that shape personal identity.#Correct. This lead introduces a clear analytical idea for readers.
~I had to write about a text and make a page.#Incorrect. This is informal and does not establish the topic.
~Here is some stuff about a book.#Incorrect. This is vague and not publication ready.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q21::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student publishes a paragraph for readers who know the text well.
Which revision may be appropriate?{
=Use less plot summary and more focused analysis of evidence.#Correct. If readers know the text, the writing can move more quickly into analysis.
~Remove all analysis because readers know the text.#Incorrect. Knowledge of the text does not remove the need for analysis.
~Use only unexplained quotations.#Incorrect. Evidence still needs explanation.
~Add unrelated background information.#Incorrect. Extra unrelated context can distract readers.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q22::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student plans to post writing to a LAN page.
Which export check is most useful?{
~Does the page include a hidden joke?#Incorrect. Hidden jokes do not support academic publication.
~Did I use a file format no one can open?#Incorrect. The file should be accessible to the intended audience.
=Are the file, links, images, and formatting readable for the intended audience?#Correct. Publication requires checking that readers can access and understand the final product.
~Can I skip the final review because the draft is online?#Incorrect. Posting does not replace quality control.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q23::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student adds a caption under an image.
Which caption is strongest?{
~This picture is interesting.#Incorrect. This is vague and does not explain the image's purpose.
~Image from the internet.#Incorrect. This does not connect the image to the writing or provide useful source detail.
~Look at this before reading.#Incorrect. This command does not explain the relationship to the writing.
=This image illustrates the crowded setting described in the paragraph and helps readers understand the character's limited choices.#Correct. This caption connects the image to the writing and audience understanding.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q24::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student has to choose between two final drafts.
Which draft is more publication ready?{
=The draft with a clear audience, revised paragraphs, purposeful multimedia, and checked conventions#Correct. These are the core features of quality writing for publication.
~The draft with the most images but weak explanation#Incorrect. Images do not compensate for weak writing.
~The draft with no title and no source notes#Incorrect. Title and source notes support publication quality.
~The draft that has never been revised#Incorrect. Revision is necessary before publication.
}

::ENG3_U06_L06_Q25::MLA.ENG3.C.03
Context: A student asks what mastery looks like in this lesson.
Which answer best matches the standard?{
~I can post any draft as long as it has one image.#Incorrect. Publication requires quality writing, audience awareness, and purposeful multimedia.
~I can make a webpage colorful without revising the writing.#Incorrect. Color does not replace revision.
=I can create and export quality writing for a specific audience with multimedia that supports understanding.#Correct. This matches the standard's focus on audience, quality writing, multimedia, and publication.
~I can avoid multimedia and audience decisions completely.#Incorrect. The standard includes audience tailoring and multimedia integration.
}
